<div class="header-banner">
    <h1>Lesson Planner – Theme # I Can Make a Mola – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<div class="section-title"><h2>Lesson Identification Table</h2></div>
<table>
    <tr>
        <td style="width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</td>
        <td style="width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td><b>Scenario:</b> The Beautiful Mola</td>
        <td><b>Theme:</b> I Can Make a Mola.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        <td></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 4px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Engage in simple exchanges about familiar topics.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 4px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> 
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Listening: Can identify the setting and main characters in simple stories about molas (e.g., 'This story is about a girl making a mola.').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can take part in exchanges about mola colors (e.g., 'What color is it?' 'It is red.').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can respond to simple questions about molas (e.g., 'Is it pretty?' 'Yes, it is colorful.').</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<div class="section-title"><h2>The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2></div>
<table>
    <tr>
        <th style="background-color: #e6e6e6; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="background-color: #e6e6e6; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="background-color: #e6e6e6; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
        </td>
        <td>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and shows real molas or large, colorful pictures of molas, pointing to different parts. The teacher asks, "What is this?" and models the word "mola." The teacher points to colors and asks, "What color is this?" encouraging students to listen and repeat or point to colors they hear. (e.g., "Listen: red, blue, yellow."). The teacher focuses on sounds /m/ in 'mola' and /k/ in 'colors' as they say the words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a simplified mola design (or picture) with a turtle. The teacher says, "This is a mola. It has a turtle. It has many colors: red, yellow, turquoise." The teacher then shows a simple mola craft project example and models the first step, saying slowly, "First, cut the fabric carefully." The teacher exaggerates the pronunciation of 'mola' (/ˈmoʊlə/) and 'colors' (/ˈkʌlərz/) and asks students to listen carefully. The teacher also models "I like this mola because it is colorful" while pointing to the mola.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a book or a mola?" (Teacher points to a mola).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does a mola have one color or many colors?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(After modeling instructions) "Do we cut the fabric first or last?"</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p>
        </td>
        <td>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio story (1-1.5 minutes) about a girl named Ana making a mola, accompanied by simple pictures or a sequence of images (slideshow). The story introduces the vocabulary for animals (turtle, butterfly, jaguar) and colors (turquoise, golden, vibrant) as well as initial steps.
            The story will contain simple sentences like: "Ana likes to make molas. First, she chooses her fabric. She likes vibrant colors. She decides to make a mola with a butterfly. Her mola has many colors: red, yellow, and turquoise. It is very unique." It will also include simple statements like "I like the butterfly because it is beautiful."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understand basic information in short, clear oral messages on familiar topics related to molas and their creation.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher plays the audio story twice.
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "Who is making a mola in the story? Is it Ana or her brother?" (Students listen and raise hand/point to picture of Ana).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students listen to statements and say "True" or "False." (e.g., "Ana makes a mola with a jaguar." - False. "Her mola has red, yellow, and turquoise colors." - True.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher shows pictures of a butterfly, a turtle, and a jaguar. "What animal does Ana choose for her mola?" (Students listen and point to the correct animal, e.g., 'butterfly').</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus)</p>
        </td>
        <td>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific information about mola colors, animals, and instructional steps. Use of Present simple for descriptions and 'because' for reasons. Vocabulary: mola, colors, turtle, butterfly, jaguar, cut, sew, paste, choose, vibrant, traditional, first, second, third, next.
            Pronunciation focus on sounds /m/ and /k/, and phonemic awareness with words like 'red,' 'orange,' 'vibrant,' 'second.'</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Listen and Circle:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with three different mola designs (e.g., one with a turtle, one with a butterfly, one with a jaguar; each with distinct color combinations). The teacher describes one mola orally: "This mola has a golden turtle. It has vibrant red and turquoise colors. It is very unique." Students listen carefully and circle the mola that matches the description. (Repeat for 2-3 molas).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Listen and Number:</b> The teacher shows a set of picture cards illustrating mola-making steps out of order (e.g., 'choose fabric', 'cut fabric', 'sew pieces', 'decorate'). The teacher plays an audio recording describing the steps: "First, choose your fabric. Second, cut it carefully. Next, sew the pieces together. Last, decorate your mola." Students listen and number the pictures in the correct sequence on their worksheet (1, 2, 3, 4). Focus on listening for 'first, second, next, last.'</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Listen and Complete (Gap-fill for colors/reasons):</b> The teacher plays short audio sentences. Students listen and complete missing words in sentences on their worksheet. E.g., Audio: "My mola is _____. I like it because it is _____." (Students fill in 'red', 'beautiful'). Another example: "This mola has a _____ butterfly. It is _____." (Students fill in 'golden', 'pretty'). This reinforces adjectives and the use of 'because'.</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task)</p>
        </td>
        <td>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Listening and Drawing. Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a blank piece of paper and a set of crayons/markers.
            The teacher plays an audio recording of a person describing their 'dream mola' or a 'mola project' they are working on. The description uses the target vocabulary (mola, vibrant, turquoise, golden, turtle, butterfly, jaguar, cut, sew, decorate) and grammar (Present simple, 'because', instructional steps) from the lesson.
            Example Audio: "Hi! My name is Carlos. I am making a mola. First, I choose a big, round piece of fabric. I want my mola to have a big, golden jaguar. I like jaguars because they are unique. Then, I cut small pieces of vibrant turquoise and red fabric to decorate around the jaguar. It will be a very traditional mola. It has many colors and shapes."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. The teacher explains the activity: "Listen to the description of a mola. Work with your partner to draw the mola Carlos describes."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. The teacher plays the audio description twice. Students listen carefully, working together to interpret the instructions and details.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. After listening, pairs collaborate to draw the mola based on the auditory information (e.g., shape, main animal, colors, decorative elements). They might confirm details with each other by pointing: "Did you hear 'round' or 'square'?" or "What color is the jaguar?" This encourages peer-to-peer listening confirmation.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Pairs briefly present their drawings, pointing to elements they heard described. (Teacher listens for their comprehension of details).</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative Assessment)</p>
        </td>
        <td>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the collaborative drawing activity, focusing on students' ability to follow auditory instructions and identify key details. Also, a quick individual 'Listen and Point' task.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete an individual "Listen and Point" worksheet. The teacher plays three very short audio descriptions of different simple molas (e.g., "This mola has a red butterfly. It is small." "This mola has a big blue turtle. It is traditional." "This mola has a yellow jaguar. It is colorful."). For each description, students point to the corresponding picture among a choice of four on their worksheet.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify main characters/animals and setting (e.g., girl making a mola, animal on mola).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate identification of colors mentioned in the descriptions.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct sequence of instructional steps in auditory input.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension of 'because' when giving reasons for preferences in listening tasks.</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket by drawing a happy face if they could understand the mola descriptions well, and a neutral or sad face if it was difficult. They also respond to:
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I heard many new words about molas today. (Yes/No)"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can hear the colors (e.g., red, turquoise) in the story. (Yes/No)"</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one new word about molas you heard today?</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to understand when listening? What was difficult?</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The listening activities were effective for identifying vocabulary and simple instructions. Students responded well to visual cues accompanying the audio. The collaborative drawing activity showed good engagement and listening comprehension in pairs. Some students needed to hear the audio multiple times for sequence activities, indicating a need for continued focus on listening for sequential markers. The pronunciation focus on /m/ and /k/ was integrated by modeling and students listening for the sounds. For next lesson, I need to provide more scaffolded listening practice before expecting independent comprehension of longer sentences, possibly with simplified choices for answers. I will also incorporate more opportunities to explicitly identify reasons using 'because'.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core mola vocabulary and simple descriptive and instructional language through listening. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will use the same vocabulary and concepts in written form, enabling students to recognize the words they heard today in print and develop reading comprehension skills about molas.</p>
        </td>
        <td><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
    </tr>
</table>